# Contributing

## Setup

```sh
git clone {{ ('https://' ~ gitlab_host ~ '/' ~ org ~ '/' ~ project_name) if org and project_name else '<repo-url>' }}
cd {{ project_name | default('<repo>', true) }}
just setup
```

`just setup` points `core.hooksPath` at this checkout, installs the pinned toolchain
from `.mise/conf.d/`, and installs the hook shims. The versions you build with are
then the versions CI builds with.

The hooks run on every commit, checking formatting, lint, secrets, and the commit
message, so a push that passed locally rarely fails CI on style. They are advisory:
`--no-verify` defeats them, and the pipeline runs the whole set again with
`prek run --all-files`.

## Making a change

1. Branch off `{{ default_branch }}`.
2. Make the change, with a test that fails without it.
3. Run `just check`.
4. Commit with a Conventional Commits subject.
5. Open a merge request.

## Commit messages

The commit-msg hook enforces Conventional Commits:

```
<type>[(<scope>)][!]: <description>
```

Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `style`, `refactor`, `perf`, `test`, `build`, `ci`,
`chore`, `revert`. A `!` marks a breaking change.

The release tooling reads these to compute the next version and write the changelog,
so the type decides whether a change ships as a patch, a minor, or a major.

## Merge requests

The pipeline has to pass. Every job runs in a stage the pipeline declares, so a
failure names which job failed.

Keep a merge request to one concern. Two unrelated changes in one branch means a
reviewer cannot approve half of it.
